1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,624
Saturn's main rings are more than two hundred and seventy thousand kilometers across.

2
00:00:05,624 --> 00:00:09,131
But in most places, they're only about ten meters thick.

3
00:00:09,631 --> 00:00:13,063
For their size, that's far thinner than a sheet of paper.

4
00:00:13,063 --> 00:00:19,565
And all of that ring material together weighs only about forty percent as much as Saturn's small moon Mimas.

5
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:22,323
Now picture rings like that around Earth.

6
00:00:22,323 --> 00:00:23,975
What would your sky look like?

7
00:00:23,975 --> 00:00:26,619
And what would they do to the planet underneath?

8
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:31,959
First, the rules.

9
00:00:31,959 --> 00:00:33,950
A ring can't sit just anywhere.

10
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:35,621
Get too close to a planet,

11
00:00:35,621 --> 00:00:41,274
and its tides pull a moon apart faster than the moon's own gravity can hold it together.

12
00:00:41,774 --> 00:00:44,250
That boundary is called the Roche limit.

13
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:48,459
For a rocky moon, it sits roughly three Earth radii from the center.

14
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:51,493
About twelve thousand kilometers above your head.

15
00:00:51,993 --> 00:00:55,956
So let's say a small moon drifted inside that line, broke apart,

16
00:00:55,956 --> 00:00:59,609
and spread into a flat, glittering disk around the equator.

17
00:00:59,609 --> 00:01:03,263
Inside the limit, the pieces can never clump back together.

18
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:07,736
Saturn's rings are about ninety-eight percent water ice,

19
00:01:07,736 --> 00:01:11,826
in pieces ranging from tiny grains to chunks as big as a house.

20
00:01:11,826 --> 00:01:14,033
That's why they shine so brightly.

21
00:01:14,533 --> 00:01:16,176
Ours would be different.

22
00:01:16,176 --> 00:01:19,529
This close to the Sun, ice slowly turns to vapor,

23
00:01:19,529 --> 00:01:22,746
so Earth's rings would be mostly rock and dust.

24
00:01:22,746 --> 00:01:23,225
Darker.

25
00:01:23,225 --> 00:01:23,704
Grayer.

26
00:01:23,704 --> 00:01:24,731
Still enormous.

27
00:01:25,231 --> 00:01:29,370
And the inner edge would have to stay above about a thousand kilometers.

28
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:34,142
Any lower, and the debris drags through the thin upper air and burns up as meteors.

29
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,452
On day one, where you live changes everything.

30
00:01:39,952 --> 00:01:45,104
Stand on the equator, in a city like Quito, and you're looking at the rings edge-on.

31
00:01:45,104 --> 00:01:49,642
A thin, bright line rising from the horizon and running straight overhead.

32
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,611
Move north, to somewhere like Washington, D.C.,

33
00:01:53,611 --> 00:01:57,847
and the rings open up into a giant arch across the southern sky.

34
00:01:58,347 --> 00:02:02,158
Like a rainbow that never fades, never moves, and never sets.

35
00:02:02,158 --> 00:02:04,908
You could find south just by looking for it,

36
00:02:04,908 --> 00:02:08,344
the way sailors once used the North Star to find north.

37
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,937
Go all the way up to the Arctic Circle, and they shrink to a hump on the horizon.

38
00:02:13,937 --> 00:02:16,452
At the poles, you wouldn't see them at all.

39
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:19,233
And at night, they don't go dark.

40
00:02:19,233 --> 00:02:23,920
Sunlight still hits the parts of the rings outside Earth's shadow, so they glow.

41
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:25,384
This is called ringshine.

42
00:02:25,884 --> 00:02:27,591
One astronomer, Caleb Scharf,

43
00:02:27,591 --> 00:02:32,595
worked out that if the rings reflected just ten percent of the sunlight hitting them,

44
00:02:32,595 --> 00:02:36,893
every square meter would shine like a hundred-and-thirty-watt light bulb.

45
00:02:37,393 --> 00:02:38,667
Nights get brighter.

46
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:40,515
Stargazing gets a lot harder.

47
00:02:40,515 --> 00:02:45,422
And every night, Earth's own shadow cuts a black gap across the glowing arch.

48
00:02:48,167 --> 00:02:51,404
Over a full year, the rings start doing something sneaky.

49
00:02:51,404 --> 00:02:52,427
They cast shadows.

50
00:02:52,927 --> 00:02:55,780
Earth is tilted, so the rings are tilted too.

51
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:00,218
In northern summer, their shadow falls across the Southern Hemisphere.

52
00:03:00,218 --> 00:03:02,311
Six months later, it swaps sides.

53
00:03:02,811 --> 00:03:04,823
Notice which half gets the shade:

54
00:03:04,823 --> 00:03:06,652
the one already having winter.

55
00:03:06,652 --> 00:03:09,578
Less sunlight, in the season that has the least.

56
00:03:09,578 --> 00:03:12,200
Winters there could get colder and harsher.

57
00:03:13,067 --> 00:03:17,519
Twice a year, at the equinoxes, the Sun lines up with the ring plane itself.

58
00:03:18,019 --> 00:03:21,433
From the equator, the rings would seem to slice the Sun in half.

59
00:03:21,933 --> 00:03:27,284
On those days, the ring's shadow shrinks to a thin stripe right along the equator.

60
00:03:27,284 --> 00:03:32,245
Then, as the seasons turn, it slowly widens again over the other hemisphere.

61
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:39,008
Scientists at Sandia National Laboratories actually modeled an Earth with an equatorial ring.

62
00:03:39,008 --> 00:03:42,820
They found it would mostly shade the tropics and subtropics,

63
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:46,377
bouncing a big share of tropical sunlight back to space.

64
00:03:46,877 --> 00:03:48,569
Their conclusion was striking:

65
00:03:48,569 --> 00:03:52,460
shading like that might be enough to help push Earth into an ice age.

66
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:54,983
Then there's space traffic.

67
00:03:54,983 --> 00:03:59,472
The International Space Station orbits about four hundred kilometers up.

68
00:03:59,472 --> 00:04:00,905
That's below the rings.

69
00:04:00,905 --> 00:04:01,217
Fine.

70
00:04:01,717 --> 00:04:05,868
GPS satellites orbit at about twenty thousand kilometers,

71
00:04:05,868 --> 00:04:12,640
and the geostationary satellites that carry TV and weather data sit near thirty-six thousand.

72
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:13,805
Above the rings.

73
00:04:13,805 --> 00:04:14,533
Also fine.

74
00:04:15,033 --> 00:04:20,351
But anything orbiting along the equator in between would be flying through a shooting gallery.

75
00:04:20,351 --> 00:04:25,387
And radio signals from satellites near the equator would have to pass through the debris.

76
00:04:25,887 --> 00:04:30,025
Rockets heading for high orbits would probably launch far from the equator,

77
00:04:30,025 --> 00:04:33,281
and climb over the ring plane rather than punch through it.

78
00:04:36,033 --> 00:04:38,706
Give it a thousand years, and people adapt.

79
00:04:38,706 --> 00:04:42,001
Unlike the Moon, the rings never move across the sky.

80
00:04:42,001 --> 00:04:43,803
They're a permanent landmark.

81
00:04:44,303 --> 00:04:47,970
The farther you are from the equator, the lower the arch sits.

82
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:52,169
So a sailor could estimate their latitude just by measuring its height.

83
00:04:52,669 --> 00:04:55,519
Every culture would grow up under the rings.

84
00:04:55,519 --> 00:04:56,491
Ring calendars.

85
00:04:56,491 --> 00:04:57,204
Ring myths.

86
00:04:57,204 --> 00:04:59,276
Maybe even ring-shadow sundials.

87
00:04:59,776 --> 00:05:01,644
Even the night would keep time.

88
00:05:01,644 --> 00:05:05,742
Earth's shadow would slide along the glowing arch from dusk to dawn,

89
00:05:05,742 --> 00:05:07,610
like the hand of a giant clock.

90
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:10,874
Astronomers, though, would not be fans.

91
00:05:10,874 --> 00:05:12,355
From much of the planet,

92
00:05:12,355 --> 00:05:16,799
a stripe of the sky is permanently hidden behind a wall of glowing rock.

93
00:05:17,299 --> 00:05:20,722
And every telescope pointed near that stripe is fighting ringshine.

94
00:05:23,467 --> 00:05:25,405
But rings don't last forever.

95
00:05:25,405 --> 00:05:28,146
Collisions grind the particles into dust.

96
00:05:28,146 --> 00:05:30,686
Some slowly spiral inward and burn up,

97
00:05:30,686 --> 00:05:34,229
a steady drizzle of shooting stars along the equator.

98
00:05:34,729 --> 00:05:36,585
Our big Moon doesn't help either.

99
00:05:36,585 --> 00:05:38,723
Its gravity keeps tugging on the ring,

100
00:05:38,723 --> 00:05:42,324
and Scharf thinks that would make long-lived rings hard to keep.

101
00:05:43,167 --> 00:05:45,264
Even Saturn's rings are temporary.

102
00:05:45,264 --> 00:05:52,790
NASA says ice from the rings is raining down into the planet fast enough to fill an Olympic swimming pool every half hour.

103
00:05:53,290 --> 00:05:57,407
By NASA's estimate, they could be gone in less than a hundred million years.

104
00:05:57,907 --> 00:06:02,290
So Earth's rings would probably fade within a few tens of millions of years.

105
00:06:02,290 --> 00:06:05,289
Which brings us to the strangest part of this story.

106
00:06:06,133 --> 00:06:07,735
This might have actually happened.

107
00:06:08,235 --> 00:06:08,813
In 2024,

108
00:06:08,813 --> 00:06:15,383
geologists at Monash University in Australia studied twenty-one impact craters from a time,

109
00:06:15,383 --> 00:06:19,065
about four hundred and sixty-six million years ago,

110
00:06:19,065 --> 00:06:22,025
when Earth was being hit unusually often.

111
00:06:22,525 --> 00:06:27,276
Every single one of them formed within about thirty degrees of the equator of the time.

112
00:06:27,776 --> 00:06:28,559
That's weird.

113
00:06:28,559 --> 00:06:34,338
About seventy percent of the land that could have preserved those craters sat outside that band.

114
00:06:34,338 --> 00:06:36,565
The odds of that happening by chance?

115
00:06:36,565 --> 00:06:38,672
Roughly one in twenty-five million.

116
00:06:39,533 --> 00:06:40,733
Their explanation:

117
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:45,199
a large asteroid passed inside Earth's Roche limit, was torn apart,

118
00:06:45,199 --> 00:06:46,398
and formed a ring.

119
00:06:46,898 --> 00:06:51,032
Then, over millions of years, chunks from that ring fell to the ground,

120
00:06:51,032 --> 00:06:52,429
mostly near the equator.

121
00:06:53,267 --> 00:06:57,574
Not long after, Earth cooled, and slid into the Hirnantian ice age,

122
00:06:57,574 --> 00:07:01,561
one of the coldest stretches of the last half a billion years.

123
00:07:02,061 --> 00:07:05,086
The team thinks the ring's shadow may have helped.

124
00:07:05,086 --> 00:07:08,293
But they're careful to say it's an idea, not a proof.

125
00:07:08,293 --> 00:07:12,650
Nobody knows yet how big that ring was, or how much sunlight it blocked.

126
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:16,711
Earth may have had a disk of debris once before, too.

127
00:07:16,711 --> 00:07:22,285
Most scientists think our Moon formed after a Mars-sized world slammed into the young Earth,

128
00:07:22,285 --> 00:07:24,709
about four and a half billion years ago.

129
00:07:25,209 --> 00:07:27,403
In the classic version of that story,

130
00:07:27,403 --> 00:07:30,071
the wreckage spread into a ring around Earth,

131
00:07:30,071 --> 00:07:32,621
and the ring gathered itself into the Moon.

132
00:07:32,621 --> 00:07:35,052
That Moon is still drifting away from us,

133
00:07:35,052 --> 00:07:37,839
about three point eight centimeters every year.

134
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:40,812
And rings are common out there.

135
00:07:40,812 --> 00:07:44,218
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune all have them.

136
00:07:44,218 --> 00:07:49,532
Even the dwarf planet Haumea and a small icy world called Chariklo have rings.

137
00:07:50,032 --> 00:07:51,458
Mars may get one next.

138
00:07:51,458 --> 00:07:55,996
Its moon Phobos is spiraling inward by about two meters every century,

139
00:07:55,996 --> 00:07:59,691
and could be torn apart in thirty to fifty million years.

140
00:08:00,533 --> 00:08:04,149
So the next time you look up at a plain, empty sky, remember:

141
00:08:04,149 --> 00:08:06,283
it might not always have been empty.

142
00:08:06,783 --> 00:08:09,968
If Earth got rings tomorrow, where would you want to live?

143
00:08:09,968 --> 00:08:11,835
Under the arch, or under the line?

144
00:08:11,835 --> 00:08:13,153
Tell me in the comments.
